WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.427
Suppose a product price is negative.

00:00:02.427 --> 00:00:06.347
It may be tempting to use ABS and make it positive.

00:00:06.347 --> 00:00:09.357
But that silently invents a correction.

00:00:09.357 --> 00:00:16.072
The negative value is evidence that something went wrong.

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:22.417
A safer workflow flags the value, preserves the source, and corrects it only when reliable evidence identifies

00:00:22.417 --> 00:00:29.907
the intended price. This principle applies to dates, phone numbers, emails, categories, and other fields.

00:00:29.907 --> 00:00:37.232
Connect this idea to a product catalog standardizing categories and quarantining invalid rows.

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:43.147
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:51.187
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:51.187 --> 00:00:57.212
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:57.212 --> 00:01:03.280
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
